A||||4||WisconsinGT||Turn signal/Cruise repacement
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-16-99||07:11 PM||||Hello, <p>I just received a new turn signal lever. After taking to a knowledgeable source, I was told to cut the cruise wire on the old lever and tape the new one to that. This would be a great way to run the wire through the steering colum. Well, it came apart after going in a little bit. So my question is this: How can I run the cruise wire down the steering colum without the old wire as a guide? Thanks!
Z||000001||mwbackus||07-17-99||02:34 AM||||Hmmm..that bites.  I would try using a thin coat hanger (ie..stiff wire) to snake down thru the column and then pull a length of nylon line through with it.  Attach the cruise wire to the nylon line and pull it through.  (you may be able to pull the cruise wire thru directly with the coat hanger, but the nylon line/cord would be preferable.)<br>Mike
Z||000002||Pretender||07-17-99||04:00 AM||||what are the steps to install the new lever?...mine has been broke ever since the winter and i don't really wanna glue it...thanks...
Z||000003||Phil||07-17-99||04:43 AM||||If you don't have cruise or don't care about it just pull on the lever (it pops out) and shove in a new one about $17. If you have cruise you have to pull the steering wheel in order to snake the control wire through. $65
Z||000004||mwbackus||07-17-99||05:27 PM||||Why would you want to remove the steering wheel to install a cruise stalk?  That does not make sense to me.  I have installed quite a few myself, and the coat hanger trick works real good if you dont have the original wire avail. for pulling a new one through.  If the old cruise wiring is present, its a piece of cake!  Just remember that nylon cord (or equivlent) needs to be used to pull the new wire through the column.<br>If this proceedure seems confusing here is a step-by-step:<p>1. Attach (ie.tie) a ~5 ft nylon cord to plug end of cruise wire located at the base of the steering column.  Wraping with electrical tape will help ensure the cord will not detach from the cruise wire.<p>2. Remove the old cruise stalk, pulling the old wire (with attached nylon cord) through the column.<p>3.  Attach the end of the nylon cord (at the top of column) to the plug end of new cruise stalk. <p>4.  Pull the new cruise wire through (from the top of the column) by pulling on the nylon cord at the base of the column, and insert the cruise stalk into the column.<p>Mike
